ate Week 2\n\nMore information\n\nImage\n\nOctober 6, 2026\n\n##  - \n\nOctober 9, 2026\n\n## Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic\n\n## OLACDE 2026 XI Energy Week\n\nMore information\n\nImage\n\nOctober 11, 2026\n\n##  - \n\nOctober 13, 2026\n\n## Riyadh, Saudi Arabia\n\n## Clean Energy Ministerial 17 - MI 11\n\nMore information\n\nImage\n\nSeptember 8, 2026\n\n##  - \n\nSeptember 8, 2026\n\n## Brussels, Belgium\n\n## 4th Brussels Hydropower Day 2026\n\nMore information\n\nImage\n\nAugust 25, 2026\n\n##  - \n\nAugust 27, 2026\n\n## Sao Paulo, Brasil\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUN Climate Chief: “the climate crisis is now a continent-wide economic security emergency” | UNFCCC (https://unfccc.int/news/un-climate-chief-the-climate-crisis-is-now-a-continent-wide-economic-security-emergency)\nciteturn985search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: today; The following is a transcript of a speech delivered by UN Climate Change Executive Secretary Simon Stiell at the European Parliament Committee on the Environment, Climate and Food Safety in Brussels, Belgium, on Thursday 10 September 2026.\n\nUN Climate Chief: “the climate crisis is now a continent-wide economic security emergency”\n\n10 September 2026\n\nUN Climate Speech\n\nImage: Simon Stiell addresses the EU Parliament Commitee\n\nCredit: Alexis Haulot | European Union\n\nImage: Simon Stiell addresses the EU Parliament Commitee\n\nCredit: Alexis Haulot | European Union\n\nThe following is a transcript of a speech delivered by UN Climate Change Executive Secretary Simon Stiell at the European Parliament Committee on the Environment, Climate and Food Safety in Brussels, Belgium, on Thursday 10 September 2026.\nThank you for this opportunity.\nClimate is sometimes seen as a partisan issue: as territory in culture wars. That is palpably false. And we’ve just seen why: Europe’s summer has been hot as hell. This is the price of global fossil fuel addiction hitting home.\nAnd it shows both that the climate crisis is now a continent-wide economic security emergency. And that doubling-down on Europe’s longstanding climate leadership is entirely in the continent’s best interest.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nC/29/3 Preliminary framework WPB 2026-2027 (https://www.irena.org/-/media/Files/IRENA/Remember/Council/Twenty-ninth-Council-meeting/C_29_3-Preliminary-Framework-for-the-Work-Programme-and-Budget-for-2026-2027.pdf)\nciteturn985search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; over 6% of these investments in 2023.Notably, Africa experienced a particularly volatile investment climate, ... 3 IRENA, COP28 Presidency, COP29 Presidency, Government of Brazil and Global Renewables Alliance, Deliveringon the UAE Consensus: Tracking progress toward tripling renewable energy capacity and doubling energy efficiency by 2030 (2024).\nC/29/3 \n \n4 448 GW (Figure 1), having grown by 585 GW, representing a 15.1% rise from the previous year. Despite \nthis positive trend, tripling renewable power capacity to 11.2 TW requires average annual additions of 1 044 \nGW between 2024 and 2030, or 16.4% compound annual growth, up from 16.1% in 2023–2030. \nFurthermore, the global distribution of renewable power capacity exhibits significant geographic disparities. \nAs of year-end 2024, Asia, Europe, and North America collectively accounted for approximately 86% of \nworldwide installed capacity. In stark contrast, the African continent accounted for only 1.5%, underscoring \nthe urgent need for targeted interventions to ensure equitable access to and deployment of renewable energy \ntechnologies across all regions. \nFigure 1: Renewable power capacity growth, 2019-2024 \n \nGW\nGW\n500\n5,000\n400\n4,000\n3,000\n300\n2,000\n200\n1,000\n100\n0\n0\n2019\n2020\n2021\n2022\n2023\n2024\nCapacity added in 2024\nSolar\nHydropower\nWind\nOthers\nSource: IRENA, Renewable capacity statistics 2024 (2025). \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIRENA\ncoalition\nFOR ACTIONACHIEVING 100%\nRENEWABLE (https://coalition.irena.org/-/media/Files/IRENA/Coalition-for-Action/Publication/IRENA_PAR_Coalition_Achieving_100_RE_2025.pdf)\nciteturn985search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; 10  Achieving 100% renewable energy by 2050THE TRIPLING TARGET ... Figure 2 Reaching the global tripling target of 11.174 TW by 2030 (left: historic annual growth rate of renewables; and right: tripling target annual growth rate)Source: (IRENA, 2025).\n10  Achieving 100% renewable energy by 2050THE TRIPLING TARGET\nAS AN ENABLER FOR\n100% RENEWABLESThe tripling target agreed upon at COP28 is of primary relevance to this paper. If this target is achieved, a total of 11.2 TW of renewable power will be installed worldwide by 2030. Given that levels were 4.4 TW at the end of 2024, growing by 585 GW at a an annual rate of 15.1%, this still falls short of the required annual renewable capacity growth rate of 16.1%, and would result in a shortfall of 3.1 TW by 2030 (IRENA, 2025). If the historic annual growth rate of renewable capacity of 10% were to continue, only 7.5 TW of renewable capacity would be installed by 2030 - far short of the COP28 tripling target (IRENA, 2024b) [see Figure 2].Figure 2 Reaching the global tripling target of 11.174 TW by 2030 (left: historic annual growth rate of renewables; and right: tripling target annual growth rate)Source: (IRENA, 2025).Tripling renewable power capacity by end of this decade is a global target. All countries, including those\nwhich have already reached significant renewable energy capacities, should contribute to the tripling\nand onwards to get to a fully renewables-based systems before 2050. Meeting the short-term tripling\ntarget does not necessarily place the world on a 100% renewable energy track by 2050. The growth\nof renewables and efficiency improvements needs to continue beyond 2030, and therefore the tripling\ntarget constitutes an initial building block towards 100% renewable energy systems.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlobal Renewables Summit 2026 - Global Renewables Alliance (https://globalrenewablesalliance.org/global-renewables-summit-2026/)\nciteturn985search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: yesterday; Building on momentum from the Santa Marta Conference, London Climate Action Week and the Electrify Now campaign, the 2026 Summit will focus on delivering the global commitment to triple renewable energy capacity by 2030 and advancing the policies, investment and partnerships needed to electrify economies.Hosted by Global Renewables Alliance, Fortescue and Climate Group, the Summit is a key milestone on the road to COP31, showcasing progress, driving implementation and reinforcing the role of renewables as the foundation of secure, competitive and affordable energy systems. ... September 24, 2026\n\n## About\n\nThe Global Renewables Summit returns to Climate Week NYC, bringing together heads of state, ministers, CEOs, investors and international organisations to accelerate the deployment of renewable-powered electrification.\n\nBuilding on momentum from the Santa Marta Conference, London Climate Action Week and the Electrify Now campaign, the 2026 Summit will focus on delivering the global commitment to triple renewable energy capacity by 2030 and advancing the policies, investment and partnerships needed to electrify economies.\n\nHosted by Global Renewables Alliance, Fortescue and Climate Group, the Summit is a key milestone on the road to COP31, showcasing progress, driving implementation and reinforcing the role of renewables as the foundation of secure, competitive and affordable energy systems.\n\n## Agenda\n\nProgramme – Invitation only \n\nPhotos\n\nMonday, 21 September\n09:30 - 12:30\n\n##### \nHigh-level Leaders Dialogue\nDelivering Electrify Now\n\nMonday, 21 September\n09:30 - 12:30\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublications (https://www.irena.org/en/publications)\nciteturn985search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; How to Decarbonise Global Transport and Industry? ... 2024 – a Year of Records for Renewables, Yet Progress Falls Short to 3xRenewables by 2030 ... Tripling Renewables is Key to Meeting Climate Goals ... Tripling Renewable Power and Doubling Energy Efficiency by 2030\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTripling renewable power capacity and doubling energy efficiecny by 2030: Crucial steps toward 1.5C (https://www.irena.org/-/media/Files/IRENA/Agency/Publication/2023/Oct/COP28_IRENA_GRA_Tripling_renewables_doubling_efficiency_2023.pdf)\nciteturn985search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.9 years ago; TRIPLING RENEWABLE POWER AND DOUBLING ENERGY EFFICIENCY BY 2030 ... It represents global perspectives within the renewable energy and climate change space, with the COP28 Presidency, the International Renewable Energy Agency (IRENA) and the Global Renewables Alliance (GRA) uniting to provide concrete recommendations on the means to meet these renewable power and energy efficiency targets.The solutions presented are technologically mature, cost-competitive and commercially available, and can be scaled up rapidly in most countries around the world; indeed, utility-scale solar PV and onshore wind are already the most cost-competitive sources of new electricity supply in most countries today (IRENA, 2023b). ... It would also improve air quality and reduce health costs; deliver universal access to clean affordable energy; and provide greater collective security and well-being.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3xRenewables - Global Renewables Alliance (https://globalrenewablesalliance.org/3xrenewables/)\nciteturn985search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years a